WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.877 --> 00:00:05.797
"Go into the room," the voice said.

00:00:05.797 --> 00:00:08.920
"It may seem like a weird
room, but that's all right

00:00:08.920 --> 00:00:10.563
because it is a weird room.

00:00:12.070 --> 00:00:14.147
Don't be afraid," the voice said.

00:00:14.147 --> 00:00:16.733
"Sit down in the chair. Drink some water.

00:00:18.630 --> 00:00:22.323
Wait for the hand and take
what the hand gives you.

00:00:23.240 --> 00:00:26.140
This," the voice said, "is your meat.

00:00:26.140 --> 00:00:28.155
Look at it and be thankful.

00:00:29.000 --> 00:00:32.890
Be thankful because this meat
did not come from an animal.

00:00:32.890 --> 00:00:36.367
No, this meat was grown in a vat."

00:00:47.410 --> 00:00:49.830
If this drone was hovering
over a slaughterhouse,

00:00:49.830 --> 00:00:51.120
you would probably turn away

00:00:51.120 --> 00:00:53.010
before watching what comes next.

00:00:53.010 --> 00:00:54.640
We like our tasty burgers.

00:00:54.640 --> 00:00:58.070
We just don't wanna think about
what went into making them.

00:00:58.070 --> 00:01:00.170
Medium rare with American cheese,

00:01:00.170 --> 00:01:02.183
but please hold the misery.

00:01:03.610 --> 00:01:05.690
This meat factory, though, is different.

00:01:05.690 --> 00:01:08.330
It's your friendly neighborhood abattoir,

00:01:08.330 --> 00:01:11.250
and it belongs to UPSIDE Foods.

00:01:11.250 --> 00:01:14.400
Meat is fundamentally the most delicious,

00:01:14.400 --> 00:01:17.810
most desired food across all cultures.

00:01:17.810 --> 00:01:18.940
And it's the center of plate

00:01:18.940 --> 00:01:21.320
for nearly everything you can imagine.

00:01:21.320 --> 00:01:22.934
People love eating meat,

00:01:22.934 --> 00:01:24.340
and we wanna be able to keep eating meat

00:01:24.340 --> 00:01:26.380
for thousands of years coming up.

00:01:26.380 --> 00:01:28.410
But the challenge we
are facing at the moment

00:01:28.410 --> 00:01:32.040
is there's just not enough
meat to be able to produce

00:01:32.040 --> 00:01:33.490
for a growing population

00:01:33.490 --> 00:01:35.390
that wants to get more and more of it.

00:01:36.660 --> 00:01:38.290
The person in charge of UPSIDE

00:01:38.290 --> 00:01:40.520
is this fella, Uma Valeti.

00:01:40.520 --> 00:01:44.010
He's not really into
killing floors of death.

00:01:44.010 --> 00:01:45.650
There's no slaughter here.

00:01:45.650 --> 00:01:47.790
And we're talking about real meat,

00:01:47.790 --> 00:01:50.700
not plant-based proteins
or meat alternatives.

00:01:50.700 --> 00:01:53.860
We're going real meat using animal cells,

00:01:53.860 --> 00:01:56.160
but not having to raise
and slaughter animals.

00:01:57.530 --> 00:01:59.870
This is the UPSIDE Foods
production facility

00:01:59.870 --> 00:02:01.080
and innovation center.

00:02:01.080 --> 00:02:04.045
What we're doing at UPSIDE
foods really is finding a way

00:02:04.045 --> 00:02:06.890
to grow real meat
directly from animal cells

00:02:06.890 --> 00:02:08.033
into the meat we love.

00:02:09.430 --> 00:02:10.500
If you look around the facility,

00:02:10.500 --> 00:02:13.880
you'll have small, medium,
large cultivators all around us.

00:02:13.880 --> 00:02:15.690
They're meant to sell one single purpose,

00:02:15.690 --> 00:02:18.130
which is to grow real meat from any animal

00:02:18.130 --> 00:02:22.293
and be able to grow that in
a matter of 10 to 12 days.

00:02:23.520 --> 00:02:26.700
Put simply, UPSIDE grows
meat in these vats.

00:02:26.700 --> 00:02:29.600
It takes animal cells,
feeds them nutrients,

00:02:29.600 --> 00:02:32.680
waits a couple of weeks,
and then digs into the vats

00:02:32.680 --> 00:02:35.750
and harvests out some pork
chops or chicken breasts.

00:02:35.750 --> 00:02:38.560
If you look at this place, I
mean, it looks like a brewery,

00:02:38.560 --> 00:02:40.497
and that's essentially what it is, right?

00:02:40.497 --> 00:02:43.030
Right, this should be
very familiar to people.

00:02:43.030 --> 00:02:45.960
When you walk to a brewery,
you see very large steel tanks.

00:02:45.960 --> 00:02:48.060
When you walk into a future
meat production facility,

00:02:48.060 --> 00:02:50.280
you should be able to see very clean tanks

00:02:50.280 --> 00:02:53.493
that are there growing meat
of any species we love to eat.

00:02:54.340 --> 00:02:55.710
These are words that make sense

00:02:55.710 --> 00:02:56.890
when put together,

00:02:56.890 --> 00:03:00.290
although the concept itself
takes some getting used to.

00:03:00.290 --> 00:03:02.170
So we'll take cells from a cow,

00:03:02.170 --> 00:03:04.740
we'll take cells from a pig
or cells from a chicken,

00:03:04.740 --> 00:03:06.550
and we'll grow them outside the animal.

00:03:06.550 --> 00:03:09.880
And we provide them with clean
and nutritious ingredients

00:03:09.880 --> 00:03:10.830
in a clean environment,

00:03:10.830 --> 00:03:14.100
and we'll let them grow
for a couple of weeks.

00:03:14.100 --> 00:03:16.730
At the end of the couple of
weeks, we'll harvest meat,

00:03:16.730 --> 00:03:18.280
which is essentially the raw meat

00:03:18.280 --> 00:03:20.790
that you would get when
an animal is slaughtered.

00:03:20.790 --> 00:03:21.623
And ultimately,

00:03:21.623 --> 00:03:23.365
you'll cook it just like
you always cooked meat.

00:03:24.410 --> 00:03:26.860
What's the largest piece
of meat you've ever eaten?

00:03:26.860 --> 00:03:28.380
Just some giant steak.

00:03:28.380 --> 00:03:29.230
40 ounces?
Yeah,

00:03:29.230 --> 00:03:31.190
I think I've probably gotten

00:03:31.190 --> 00:03:33.060
to the 40 range, yeah.
40 ounces, okay.

00:03:33.060 --> 00:03:36.490
You had to grow a 2000-pound steer.

00:03:36.490 --> 00:03:37.323
Yeah.

00:03:37.323 --> 00:03:39.620
And then cut it back down to 40 ounces.

00:03:39.620 --> 00:03:41.950
Now imagine what happens in the reverse.

00:03:41.950 --> 00:03:44.320
We're building that same
meat from the building blocks

00:03:44.320 --> 00:03:47.820
of cells much faster than
what it takes a beef cattle

00:03:47.820 --> 00:03:49.120
to grow, which takes two years.

00:03:49.120 --> 00:03:52.010
That's really why the resource
use for cultivated meat

00:03:52.010 --> 00:03:54.900
is 10 times lower for water use.

00:03:54.900 --> 00:03:56.410
Our greenhouse gas emissions compared

00:03:56.410 --> 00:03:59.170
to growing an entire animal for two years,

00:03:59.170 --> 00:04:01.820
and then cutting it back
down to this 40-ounce steak.

00:04:03.520 --> 00:04:06.110
UPSIDE has raised more than $200 million

00:04:06.110 --> 00:04:08.540
from the likes of Bill
Gates, Richard Branson,

00:04:08.540 --> 00:04:10.890
and food giants, like Tyson and Cargill.

00:04:10.890 --> 00:04:13.410
They're all betting that the
company will crack the code

00:04:13.410 --> 00:04:15.280
on making lots of meat without needing

00:04:15.280 --> 00:04:17.125
to do any slaughtering.

00:04:17.125 --> 00:04:20.764
That sound you hear is pigs and
chickens and cows rejoicing.

00:04:29.630 --> 00:04:33.620
Uma has graciously allowed me
passage into his meat palace,

00:04:33.620 --> 00:04:37.110
where we shall find out
how UPSIDE makes its meat.

00:04:37.110 --> 00:04:38.780
Okay, so the feed starts here?

00:04:38.780 --> 00:04:40.530
Yup, so if we're looking at two tanks.

00:04:40.530 --> 00:04:41.740
This is a smaller tank

00:04:41.740 --> 00:04:44.060
and this is a larger tank
that is holding the feed.

00:04:44.060 --> 00:04:46.050
So we're looking at ingredients

00:04:46.050 --> 00:04:48.350
that are being mixed in
here in a particular recipe.

00:04:48.350 --> 00:04:50.560
See this little fanlike thing?

00:04:50.560 --> 00:04:51.393
Yeah.

00:04:51.393 --> 00:04:52.226
That's the agitator in there-

00:04:52.226 --> 00:04:54.080
Okay.
That kind of mixes slowly,

00:04:54.080 --> 00:04:57.060
mixes so the ingredients
are nicely dissolved,

00:04:57.060 --> 00:04:59.807
and that becomes easier
for the cells to absorb it.

00:04:59.807 --> 00:05:02.620
And the feeds coming more from like a farm

00:05:02.620 --> 00:05:04.570
or like a chemical?

00:05:04.570 --> 00:05:07.530
It comes from agricultural plants.

00:05:07.530 --> 00:05:09.640
It could be corn. It could be soy.

00:05:09.640 --> 00:05:12.170
It could be other plants that
people are just processing

00:05:12.170 --> 00:05:14.290
to make into powders like flour mills.

00:05:14.290 --> 00:05:16.930
So we have a milling area where
we mill some of the powders.

00:05:16.930 --> 00:05:17.763
Okay.

00:05:17.763 --> 00:05:19.233
So that it can easily mix into the water,

00:05:19.233 --> 00:05:21.683
then the cells can start
absorbing the nutrients.

00:05:22.970 --> 00:05:25.570
Here, we are coming over to the first

00:05:25.570 --> 00:05:26.700
of our seed cultivators.

00:05:26.700 --> 00:05:29.450
We have multiple of them, but
let's use one as an example.

00:05:29.450 --> 00:05:31.320
So this is a seed cultivator.

00:05:31.320 --> 00:05:33.760
You grow the thimble of
cells into like a flask.

00:05:33.760 --> 00:05:35.800
Once it gets to a reasonable-sized flask,

00:05:35.800 --> 00:05:37.850
we have a sterile
connection from that flask

00:05:37.850 --> 00:05:38.780
into this cultivator.

00:05:38.780 --> 00:05:39.613
Okay.

00:05:39.613 --> 00:05:40.990
And we'll pump the cells in there

00:05:40.990 --> 00:05:43.620
and then feed comes in
and it starts mixing.

00:05:43.620 --> 00:05:46.530
So there's literally a chicken pipeline

00:05:46.530 --> 00:05:49.970
that's ferrying stuff from
one of these vats to the next.

00:05:49.970 --> 00:05:52.480
Yeah, and the cells are
naturally programmed to double,

00:05:52.480 --> 00:05:55.370
like I said, every 18, 24, 36 hours.

00:05:55.370 --> 00:05:58.290
And they're doubling and doubling
and doubling and doubling,

00:05:58.290 --> 00:06:00.790
and pretty quickly, in
10, 20, 30 doublings,

00:06:00.790 --> 00:06:04.060
you're gonna get really
highly dense cells in here

00:06:04.060 --> 00:06:06.690
that become, say if it's chicken,

00:06:06.690 --> 00:06:08.210
that's chicken growing in there.

00:06:08.210 --> 00:06:09.090
When you harvest it out,

00:06:09.090 --> 00:06:11.120
it's going to be like
a really dense liquid.

00:06:11.120 --> 00:06:14.080
What you do then is you
separate out the water from it.

00:06:14.080 --> 00:06:17.480
And it just starts
becoming like the raw meat

00:06:17.480 --> 00:06:18.670
that you're used to seeing,

00:06:18.670 --> 00:06:22.830
but it doesn't have the marbling

00:06:22.830 --> 00:06:24.080
or so when it comes out of here.

00:06:24.080 --> 00:06:26.140
Yeah, like what color? It's pinkish or?

00:06:26.140 --> 00:06:27.018
It's pinkish, yeah.

00:06:27.018 --> 00:06:27.851
It's pinkish, okay.
It's pinkish

00:06:27.851 --> 00:06:29.800
because the cells naturally
have a little bit of a pink hue.

00:06:29.800 --> 00:06:30.830
Once you take it out,

00:06:30.830 --> 00:06:33.060
you take it into the
formulations area in the kitchen

00:06:33.060 --> 00:06:35.010
and you start making sausages, burgers,

00:06:35.010 --> 00:06:35.990
and hot dogs, and nuggets.

00:06:35.990 --> 00:06:36.823
That's crazy.

00:06:36.823 --> 00:06:37.677
So that's the step.

00:06:40.108 --> 00:06:43.220
So these are the big,
big daddy cultivators.

00:06:43.220 --> 00:06:45.240
Yeah, you can say these are
the big daddy cultivators

00:06:45.240 --> 00:06:47.680
for all cut meats.

00:06:47.680 --> 00:06:49.700
These are the largest of
their kind in the world

00:06:49.700 --> 00:06:50.610
in any field.

00:06:50.610 --> 00:06:53.467
And what they do is be able
to produce the textures

00:06:53.467 --> 00:06:55.650
and the cuts you love from cold cut meats.

00:06:55.650 --> 00:06:57.840
I get more confused on the properties.

00:06:57.840 --> 00:06:59.663
Like when you cut into a chicken breast,

00:06:59.663 --> 00:07:01.610
it's kinda like fibrous.

00:07:01.610 --> 00:07:04.420
And I still can't picture in my head

00:07:04.420 --> 00:07:07.110
how you guys get the when
you start combining things.

00:07:07.110 --> 00:07:08.790
Is it just there's machines

00:07:08.790 --> 00:07:11.680
that like sort of mush this stuff together

00:07:11.680 --> 00:07:13.660
in certain recipes, and then?

00:07:13.660 --> 00:07:15.290
There's certainly a role for machines,

00:07:15.290 --> 00:07:17.660
but the majority of the work
that we've done at UPSIDE

00:07:17.660 --> 00:07:19.320
is actually having the cells figure out

00:07:19.320 --> 00:07:20.540
how to make the tissues.

00:07:20.540 --> 00:07:22.920
Between here and actually
getting in front of a consumer,

00:07:22.920 --> 00:07:24.400
it'll get into the shape of the breast.

00:07:24.400 --> 00:07:26.520
That forming and shaping
of it happens after it

00:07:26.520 --> 00:07:27.674
from the solid meat that we get.

00:07:27.674 --> 00:07:29.356
Okay, and that's just
done with like a machine

00:07:29.356 --> 00:07:30.189
that shapes it?
That's how it's done.

00:07:30.189 --> 00:07:31.022
That's a machine that shapes it.

00:07:31.022 --> 00:07:32.380
But otherwise, it's
coming out of this vat,

00:07:32.380 --> 00:07:33.213
more or less.
Yes.

00:07:33.213 --> 00:07:34.627
Okay, okay.
Yeah, yeah.

00:07:34.627 --> 00:07:35.940
And we have all kinds of cells.

00:07:35.940 --> 00:07:40.090
We have what we call
fibroblast. We have myoblasts.

00:07:40.090 --> 00:07:42.740
And there's other cells that
accumulate a lot of fat.

00:07:43.650 --> 00:07:45.080
And we're trying to play with the taste

00:07:45.080 --> 00:07:46.283
and the textures of each of those cells

00:07:46.283 --> 00:07:48.500
when you put it into a format of a meat.

00:07:48.500 --> 00:07:49.333
We're trying to build it

00:07:49.333 --> 00:07:51.500
from the most fundamental building blocks.

00:07:51.500 --> 00:07:53.490
UPSIDE started in 2015

00:07:53.490 --> 00:07:56.000
and used to go by the name Memphis Meats.

00:07:56.000 --> 00:07:58.400
Over the years, it's been joined by dozens

00:07:58.400 --> 00:08:00.610
of startups also trying to grow meat

00:08:00.610 --> 00:08:02.880
in big metal containers.

00:08:02.880 --> 00:08:04.540
Uma's big flex is that UPSIDE

00:08:04.540 --> 00:08:07.490
has moved past the laboratory R&amp;D phase,

00:08:07.490 --> 00:08:08.560
and is well on its way

00:08:08.560 --> 00:08:11.690
to opening a huge synthetic meat factory.

00:08:11.690 --> 00:08:14.400
This building near Berkeley,
California was just finished

00:08:14.400 --> 00:08:16.770
and will soon pump out engineered protein

00:08:16.770 --> 00:08:19.440
at unprecedented volumes.

00:08:19.440 --> 00:08:22.810
What we're targeting is
making 50,000 pounds a year.

00:08:22.810 --> 00:08:23.643
Okay.

00:08:23.643 --> 00:08:24.570
With the capacity to go all the way up

00:08:24.570 --> 00:08:27.590
to almost half a million pounds,
like 400,000 pounds plus.

00:08:27.590 --> 00:08:29.100
But this is basically the one

00:08:29.100 --> 00:08:31.510
that shows them all how
meat production can be done.

00:08:31.510 --> 00:08:33.880
From there, we use that as
a blueprint to start saying,

00:08:33.880 --> 00:08:35.700
okay, now let's build
a production facility

00:08:35.700 --> 00:08:37.910
that's purely focused on only production.

00:08:37.910 --> 00:08:39.250
We don't need to do innovation there.

00:08:39.250 --> 00:08:40.990
That's the one we start targeting this 10

00:08:40.990 --> 00:08:42.240
to 20 million pounds.

00:08:42.240 --> 00:08:44.410
That's our next production
facility that we wanna build.

00:08:44.410 --> 00:08:45.610
Okay. This is crazy.

00:08:45.610 --> 00:08:48.470
So if a number of you guys
are successful at this,

00:08:48.470 --> 00:08:50.870
there's gonna be competitions
over just who could

00:08:50.870 --> 00:08:53.080
make the most delicious

00:08:53.080 --> 00:08:55.190
meat, right, with their process, and-

00:08:55.190 --> 00:08:58.610
Yeah, I long for that day of
saying I'm actually competing

00:08:58.610 --> 00:09:00.650
with another company in this space.

00:09:00.650 --> 00:09:04.483
It's gonna be like Coke versus
Pepsi but with like steaks.

00:09:06.910 --> 00:09:08.930
Uma's assortment of meat receptacles

00:09:08.930 --> 00:09:11.030
and slurry pipelines was impressive.

00:09:11.030 --> 00:09:13.190
But by God, I'd signed a waiver

00:09:13.190 --> 00:09:16.370
and given UPSIDE power of
attorney over my bowels.

00:09:16.370 --> 00:09:18.954
I wanted to taste some DIY chicken.

00:09:20.450 --> 00:09:21.800
How many tastings...

00:09:21.800 --> 00:09:22.633
You must've...

00:09:22.633 --> 00:09:23.690
Do you eat this at home

00:09:23.690 --> 00:09:25.520
with the kids and?
Yes.

00:09:25.520 --> 00:09:29.287
My kids, my wife, our dog.

00:09:29.287 --> 00:09:31.634
Yeah.
So we are the first family

00:09:31.634 --> 00:09:34.971
in the world to eat cultivated meat.

00:09:34.971 --> 00:09:37.430
And can the dog tell?

00:09:37.430 --> 00:09:39.970
Oh, I mean, he'll come running towards it,

00:09:39.970 --> 00:09:40.803
right?
Yeah.

00:09:40.803 --> 00:09:41.636
He just like...

00:09:41.636 --> 00:09:45.260
And you guys picked
chicken, I would presume,

00:09:45.260 --> 00:09:46.710
'cause a lot of people eat it,

00:09:46.710 --> 00:09:50.630
and this would be the
biggest early market.

00:09:50.630 --> 00:09:53.130
You would have to do chicken
or pork, pretty much, right?

00:09:53.130 --> 00:09:55.990
Chicken is something that
everybody knows how it tastes.

00:09:55.990 --> 00:09:58.147
It's one of the most
desirable meats in the world

00:09:58.147 --> 00:10:00.500
and number one in the world
and number one in the US.

00:10:00.500 --> 00:10:03.358
So we're like, we're gonna
put that on the table.

00:10:03.358 --> 00:10:05.800
I mean, that's the funny
thing is whenever you eat some

00:10:05.800 --> 00:10:08.010
of the vegetarian stuff, it's always like,

00:10:08.010 --> 00:10:10.523
oh, how does this compare to meat?

00:10:11.400 --> 00:10:12.990
I mean, this is just meat.

00:10:12.990 --> 00:10:14.590
Yeah, this is just meat, yeah.

00:10:15.980 --> 00:10:19.410
I mean, growing up, I grew
up eating a lot of meat.

00:10:19.410 --> 00:10:21.100
My dad's a veterinarian.

00:10:21.100 --> 00:10:24.440
I went to his work and was
always around cattle or sheep

00:10:24.440 --> 00:10:28.823
and kind of learned the
love for animals from that.

00:10:29.750 --> 00:10:32.120
But loved the taste of
meat, kept eating it.

00:10:32.120 --> 00:10:34.230
We had chickens in our backyard.

00:10:34.230 --> 00:10:36.070
And I had the experience of one day,

00:10:36.070 --> 00:10:37.360
the chicken I liked disappeared.

00:10:37.360 --> 00:10:38.917
And I'm like, "Where did it go?"

00:10:38.917 --> 00:10:40.260
"We just ate it last night."

00:10:40.260 --> 00:10:43.960
And I remember really crying that day.

00:10:43.960 --> 00:10:47.720
Was hard to process, so here we are.

00:10:47.720 --> 00:10:50.923
I mean, we can have the
chicken and eat it, too.

00:10:52.300 --> 00:10:53.280
It's golden-brown.

00:10:53.280 --> 00:10:56.300
You expect the mild reaction
that happening in a pan.

00:10:56.300 --> 00:10:58.210
When you're in the room, you're
starting to smell the meat.

00:10:58.210 --> 00:11:00.260
Oh, yeah, 100%. It smells
like grilled chicken.

00:11:00.260 --> 00:11:01.720
Yes.

00:11:01.720 --> 00:11:02.710
And then as you cut,

00:11:02.710 --> 00:11:05.610
he's cutting against
the bias of the fibers.

00:11:05.610 --> 00:11:07.437
And you can see that knife is actually,

00:11:07.437 --> 00:11:08.810
he had to work hard to cut it.

00:11:08.810 --> 00:11:10.720
It just doesn't go to mush.

00:11:10.720 --> 00:11:11.581
Yeah.

00:11:11.581 --> 00:11:12.883
It's not like you're cutting tofu.

00:11:15.060 --> 00:11:16.570
Based on everything I read about you guys,

00:11:16.570 --> 00:11:18.600
everyone's said it
actually tastes like meat,

00:11:18.600 --> 00:11:21.310
so my expectations are high.

00:11:21.310 --> 00:11:24.053
Okay, all right.

00:11:27.320 --> 00:11:30.570
Yeah, I mean, this is...

00:11:32.900 --> 00:11:34.742
I mean, it's meat.

00:11:34.742 --> 00:11:39.742
That's all right.

00:11:40.070 --> 00:11:42.480
I mean, it totally tastes
like grilled chicken

00:11:42.480 --> 00:11:43.860
