more tender, more moist,

00:12:02.010 --> 00:12:03.393
and tender fibers, so yeah.

00:12:07.720 --> 00:12:09.858
That's delicious. Yeah, well done.

00:12:13.510 --> 00:12:16.790
But you, otherwise, you
were gonna be a doctor,

00:12:16.790 --> 00:12:18.470
I mean, for rest of your life.

00:12:18.470 --> 00:12:20.890
Yeah, I practiced as a
cardiologist for 10 years.

00:12:20.890 --> 00:12:23.520
I think the calling was
always there from as a child.

00:12:23.520 --> 00:12:28.270
I really wanted to be able
to make a meaningful change

00:12:28.270 --> 00:12:29.150
or a difference in the world.

00:12:29.150 --> 00:12:31.440
And I couldn't think of a much better way

00:12:31.440 --> 00:12:35.410
of using my skill sets to
say if we can use science

00:12:35.410 --> 00:12:38.200
and technology we're using to grow food,

00:12:38.200 --> 00:12:42.830
that could save billions of
humans and trillions of animals.

00:12:42.830 --> 00:12:45.640
And I felt like I could
continue practicing cardiology

00:12:45.640 --> 00:12:48.190
for another 30 years and
save two or 3,000 lives.

00:12:48.190 --> 00:12:50.560
But if this works, my gosh,

00:12:50.560 --> 00:12:54.770
like it just literally would
be a giant leap for humanity.

00:12:54.770 --> 00:12:58.180
And walking away from
cardiology was not easy.

00:12:58.180 --> 00:13:01.300
And my mom and dad had,
my mom more than my dad,

00:13:01.300 --> 00:13:03.860
had a reaction of like, "You
can't walk away from that.

00:13:03.860 --> 00:13:05.450
You worked so hard from it

00:13:05.450 --> 00:13:08.110
and you're doing really well."

00:13:08.110 --> 00:13:10.610
But they also saw from I was little

00:13:10.610 --> 00:13:13.340
how much I cared about meat
and how it came to the table

00:13:13.340 --> 00:13:16.563
and running behind my dad and
playing with all the animals.

00:13:18.150 --> 00:13:19.580
They immediately got it.

00:13:19.580 --> 00:13:22.110
And my dad was an incredibly supportive,

00:13:22.110 --> 00:13:24.050
the kindest person that I've known.

00:13:24.050 --> 00:13:27.030
The only shirt he would
wear for the last five years

00:13:27.030 --> 00:13:29.570
is a shirt that I sent him, said, hey,

00:13:29.570 --> 00:13:30.950
we used to be called Memphis Meats,

00:13:30.950 --> 00:13:33.313
and then he always used
to wear that shirt.

00:13:34.490 --> 00:13:36.800
Twist of fate with COVID,

00:13:36.800 --> 00:13:40.443
my dad passed away last year.
Oh, shit, yeah.

00:13:41.360 --> 00:13:43.120
My dad was gonna be here for the opening.

00:13:43.120 --> 00:13:43.953
Yeah.

00:13:45.060 --> 00:13:46.040
So that's why it's emotional.

00:13:46.040 --> 00:13:48.940
Yeah. Yeah, no, no, no, I understand.

00:13:48.940 --> 00:13:50.833
Well, I mean,

00:13:52.980 --> 00:13:56.930
it's about as impressive a
thing as you can do, right,

00:13:56.930 --> 00:13:59.283
to shake up something this dramatic.

00:14:02.570 --> 00:14:05.950
UPSIDE's technology is
admirable and impressive.

00:14:05.950 --> 00:14:09.350
The company, though, still
faces a couple of major tests.

00:14:09.350 --> 00:14:13.460
First, the FDA has to approve
this type of cell-based meat.

00:14:13.460 --> 00:14:16.110
And second, consumers
actually have to want it

00:14:16.110 --> 00:14:18.383
when they walk down supermarket aisles.

00:14:19.400 --> 00:14:21.600
Let's talk about the price for a moment.

00:14:21.600 --> 00:14:23.620
When you guys started, I mean, I think-

00:14:23.620 --> 00:14:24.945
We were at $18,000.

00:14:24.945 --> 00:14:27.383
18,000 for how much?

00:14:27.383 --> 00:14:28.420
For a pound.

00:14:28.420 --> 00:14:29.253
For a pound of meat.

00:14:29.253 --> 00:14:32.000
So what has been the curve down over time?

00:14:32.000 --> 00:14:34.950
So I'd say we've lowered
it by about three orders

00:14:34.950 --> 00:14:36.870
of magnitude by about 1,000x.

00:14:36.870 --> 00:14:37.703
Okay.

00:14:37.703 --> 00:14:39.370
And so that brings us into the territory

00:14:39.370 --> 00:14:43.680
of being able to say we
can do premium meat pricing

00:14:43.680 --> 00:14:46.970
as we start getting ready
for producing meat at scale.

00:14:46.970 --> 00:14:48.870
10, $20 a plate.

00:14:48.870 --> 00:14:53.350
When you talk about just
one chicken breast or so,

00:14:53.350 --> 00:14:56.430
or a chicken tender,
it's gonna be under a $5.

00:14:56.430 --> 00:14:58.530
This is a lot of progress in five years,

00:14:59.640 --> 00:15:00.520
five or six years.

00:15:00.520 --> 00:15:02.350
But there is a camp of people

00:15:02.350 --> 00:15:05.720
who think this just cannot
be done economically.

00:15:05.720 --> 00:15:08.050
So we're standing on a track record

00:15:08.050 --> 00:15:10.780
of being able to question the impossible

00:15:10.780 --> 00:15:12.160
and keep moving beyond that.

00:15:12.160 --> 00:15:15.250
What I'm telling our own team

00:15:15.250 --> 00:15:17.480
that's literally broken
nearly every barrier

00:15:17.480 --> 00:15:19.420
people said we couldn't do in
the last five years, right,

00:15:19.420 --> 00:15:22.040
every single one that we
are talking about today

00:15:22.040 --> 00:15:24.160
was a much higher barrier five years ago.

00:15:24.160 --> 00:15:26.210
The investors that are
coming in here recognize

00:15:26.210 --> 00:15:27.690
that this is a long arc.

00:15:27.690 --> 00:15:28.880
And in order to create the kind

00:15:28.880 --> 00:15:30.280
of change you're talking about,

00:15:30.280 --> 00:15:33.100
this is gonna take five
years plus, 10 years plus.

00:15:33.100 --> 00:15:35.490
We are set up at a very
interesting moment in history

00:15:35.490 --> 00:15:39.730
where the trends of consumers,
the current generation,

00:15:39.730 --> 00:15:43.110
are all asking questions about
where's our food coming from

00:15:43.110 --> 00:15:45.040
and we want to demand a much better way

00:15:45.040 --> 00:15:46.790
of bringing food to the table.

00:15:46.790 --> 00:15:50.110
The current meat production
is 750 billion pounds.

00:15:50.110 --> 00:15:52.290
That's about 70 billion animals.

00:15:52.290 --> 00:15:54.610
Now that demand is approximately doubling.

00:15:54.610 --> 00:15:59.090
Let's say by 2050, we need
to have 150 billion animals

00:15:59.090 --> 00:16:00.890
on the planet to produce meat enough

00:16:00.890 --> 00:16:02.410
for everybody's demands.

00:16:02.410 --> 00:16:03.860
And it's very clear.

00:16:03.860 --> 00:16:06.900
There not enough space.
There's not enough water.

00:16:06.900 --> 00:16:08.940
And we cannot afford to have the level

00:16:08.940 --> 00:16:11.040
of greenhouse gas emissions and runoffs

00:16:11.040 --> 00:16:12.900
that come from that
much amount of animals.

00:16:12.900 --> 00:16:16.120
If we are successful and
the field is successful,

00:16:16.120 --> 00:16:18.440
it's just an enormous opportunity.

00:16:18.440 --> 00:16:20.727
It's game over for many of the
problems we're talking about.

